f every  single cell type  in your tissue of interest  so to go about doing this in the human  brain uh we  turned our attention to a sort of  previously published single cell  transcriptomic atlas  of the human cortex this wonderful paper  from the gesturing lab  did exactly this at a sort of a  gestational age that matches what we did  in our single  in our wta profiling in their wonderful  study you can see that's  in the plots in panel b that they have  actually identified  over 20 different major cell types that  make up the cortex  covering both the known subtypes of  neural progenitors from the germinal  centers  as well as different types of neurons  from the cortical plates  our idea in our workflow is that we take  this  dissociative single thyronyx catalyst  that was published  we integrate we take each one of these  cell types and then we map  their locations in our geomx wta data  across the cortical depth  and what this allows you to do then is  across each of our regions of interest  across cortical depth from the germinal  center so the cortical plate  we can identify both the proportions  and the densities of each cell type in  these data sets  so some controls before we actually dive  deep into this uh what you're seeing now  is  uh cell to location mapping results of  one subtypes  it's the outer radioglia which is the  neural stem cell that is concentrated in  this region called  osvz and if you are if you look at  our cell to location results you can see  that indeed we have successfully  mapped this cell type specifically  to the uh outer sub-ventricular zone in  the general center  a quick technical note that we'll make  here is that uh  the soft location version that we used  here  was actually modified was adapted to  work best with wta data  if we were to take the versions of cell  to location  that are designed or catered for other  technologies such as vision  we still get good sort of mapping of  radioglio to the outer subventricular  zone  but we actually start seeing sort of  background mapping to other brain  regions  such as the cortical plate which is sort  of boxed in  uh with the dashed uh diagram on the  second  so it is actually important if you're  using these  sort of self-location-like integration  tools it's important to use the right  kind of tool  that is adapted to your technology  so i'll just jump you i'll just walk you  quickly now  through our cell type mapping results in  the fetal cortex  so what you're seeing here now is a cell  density map of each of the 17 cell types  uh  identified in the single crnaseq data  across our wta data sets  as i did previously the x-axis shows you  the  sort of the spatial position of  each segmental region of interest across  cortical depth  from right to from left to right it's  going as general center the cortical  plate  and the size of the different dots for  each cell type  show you actually their predicted  density and  the output that we're talking about here  is actually compared  to a almost a manual count of cells in  that region of interest  so when we say that we think that there  are 3  thousand cells of one cell type a region  of interest we actually base that  both on self-location mapping as well as  the total number of nuclei identified  there  based on imaging so the  results of this mapping were largely a  success we were really delighted to see  that both the neural progenitors and  differentiating neurons were  successfully mapped to where they are  supposed to be  the kind of the two highlights that i'd  like to present you today  is that this mapping is really sensitive  enough to actually call  different subtypes so if we were now to  focus on  neural stem cells different types of  neural stem cells that are  located in the ventricular zone versus  the outer subventricular zone which are  the green and the red cells diagram in  the top  you actually see that sulfur location  predicts them to be in spatially  different areas of the terminal centers  in a similar manner when we look at the  spatial positions of neurons across  different steps of their differentiation  we see this really wonderful spatial  gradient we see that newborn neurons  identified as xn are located in the  germinal centers  the migrating neurons are sort of found  transiting between the adrenal centers  and the cortical plate  and then the differentiated neurons  which are indicated as mu and dp1  are nicely located in a cortical plate  suggesting that the cell type mapping  has the sensitivity to be able  to distinguish the special positions of  finely related  fine stages of neural differentiation  finally the advantage of doing  uh something like the wta assay in a way  i think the  sort of one of the major advantages over  other methods is that it's high  throughput  uh the data that i showed you before uh  or  showed you so far was generated at one  age during fetal development that's 19  post-conception weeks  with the throughput of the wta assay we  could easily start looking at  uh tissue from donors from other  other ages in fetal development and here  now what you're seeing is the cell type  mapping comparing 14  to 19 post conception weeks and when you  look at these maps there's really a  huge wealth of information that you can  identify for example between 14 and 19  weeks  you can sort of see that's both  qualitatively and quantitatively  we get this big increase in the number  of interneurons that  are colonizing the ventricular zone the  cortex  and at this time period we also see a  big increase  in the number of neurons excitatory  neurons that are accumulating in the  cortical plate  showing you that we can actually get uh  really nice comparative information  using this methodology  finally what i'll close by is uh  the sort of uh the next level version of  wa technology  uh so far i told you about doing the  bulk wta  bulk region profiling and the single  slot deconvolution  but as i said a