e use umis to make sure we sequence  uh single molecules  um so the umi is 14 bases in length  and the actual target specific part of  the barcode is  as 12 bases in length so  how many targets do they cover so having  12 bases for the target specific part  well i just did the calculation very  quickly that gives you a theoretically  16 million possibilities  but since omar is just describing  already we're doing whole transcriptome  covering over 18 000  genes so that's as many targets that we  can cover we can cover the whole  transcript too  yes thanks for the question  thank you so much and omar this question  is for you can the wta  assay offer single cell resolution  we haven't tested this explicitly but we  think so so i showed data  where uh the wta assay offers you five  cell resolution  where we can already identify the  expression of  four thousand genes and five cells um  the geo max dsp paper that came out  earlier this year in nature biotech i  think showed that  you can measure and rudy can correct me  here uh i think it showed that you can  measure  protein expression at single slot  resolution but it shied a little bit  away from rna  i think based on what we saw for five  cells i think a single cell  is most likely in the sort of  reach of wta assay the questions will be  how consistent will be the single cell  readings uh  and from different tissues how they  would scale to each other but it's  definitely something worth trying  thank you very much and i want to thank  our audience members for these great  questions coming in  we actually have time for one more  question you mentioned that  cell type specific wta  and single cell rna sequence  correlate but not perfectly how could  this correlation  be improved that's a great question  so um i think there are sort of couple  different dimensions there  so the first dimension is that uh in our  experiments we actually have like a  while you know the single scientific and  wta data set  match roughl